<%- include('../partials/layout-start') %>

<%- include('../partials/page-header', {
  icon: 'bi-signpost-split',
  heading: 'Tax advisory',
  sub: 'We introduce you to somebody who knows your jurisdiction. We do not calculate anything.'
}) %>

<section class="glass-section pb-5">
<div class="container">
  <%- include('../partials/flash') %>

  <div class="row g-4">
    <div class="col-lg-7">
      <div class="glass-card h-100"><div class="card-body p-4">
        <h2 class="h5 mb-3">What this is</h2>
        <p>
          Consultants who work across borders, or who have just gone contracting, usually
          have the same first question: how should I actually be set up? It is a good
          question and it does not have a general answer — it depends on where you are
          tax-resident, what kind of entity you work through, your VAT position, and how
          you pay yourself.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0">
          So we do the one useful thing a marketplace can do here. You describe your
          situation, we tell you who we think should read it, and if you agree we put you
          in touch. Everything after that is between you and them.
        </p>
      </div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-5">
      <div class="glass-card h-100"><div class="card-body p-4">
        <h2 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted-2 mb-3">What we do</h2>
        <ul class="list-unstyled mb-4">
          <% promise.does.forEach(function (line) { %>
            <li class="d-flex gap-2 mb-2">
              <i class="bi bi-check-circle-fill text-success flex-shrink-0 mt-1"></i>
              <span><%= line %></span>
            </li>
          <% }); %>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="h6 text-uppercase text-muted-2 mb-3">What we do not do</h2>
        <ul class="list-unstyled mb-0">
          <% promise.doesNot.forEach(function (line) { %>
            <li class="d-flex gap-2 mb-2">
              <i class="bi bi-x-circle-fill text-danger flex-shrink-0 mt-1"></i>
              <span><%= line %></span>
            </li>
          <% }); %>
        </ul>
      </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%
    /*
     * The honest replacement for the reference implementation's savings calculator.
     *
     * That calculator asked for your gross and net, assumed your entire burden becomes a
     * flat percentage of gross after restructuring, and printed the difference as a
     * monthly and annual saving. The percentage was an admin setting. A number that looks
     * like tax advice but is a guess is worse than no number, because people act on it.
     *
     * What is offered instead is a calculator that computes what it can actually know:
     * days, costs and a net BEFORE tax, with every non-billable day named. It says
     * plainly that tax is not in it.
     */
  %>
  <div class="glass-card mt-4"><div class="card-body p-4">
    <div class="row align-items-center g-3">
      <div class="col-md-8">
        <h2 class="h5 mb-2">Looking for a number?</h2>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted-2">
          We will not show you a saving. What we can show you is the arithmetic that does
          not depend on your jurisdiction: billable days after holiday, illness, bench and
          admin, your costs, and what is left before tax. The rate calculator does that,
          it is public, and it keeps nothing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="col-md-4 text-md-end">
        <a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="/rates/calculator">
          <i class="bi bi-calculator"></i> Open the rate calculator
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div></div>

  <div class="text-center mt-5">
    <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/tax-advisory/enquire">
      <i class="bi bi-envelope"></i> Ask about your setup
    </a>
    <p class="small text-muted-2 mt-3 mb-0">
      Six questions. We keep your enquiry for <%= retentionDays %> days and then delete it.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<%- include('../partials/layout-end') %>
